t  learning slash bandit problem  and we want to learn a dialogue policy  that tells us what alex i should say  next to maximize some measure of test  success  and more specifically instead of  building some kind of user simulator we  want to learn from live log data  um  and so to summarize we want to learn a  target policy from data collected using  a logging policy  and this is part of a larger offline  policy optimization framework  in which um we start by collecting some  randomized uh conversational data with  live users  then we make sure we log the state the  action the action probability and the  reward at every step of the conversation  and once you have collected data we can  use that to do offline policy  optimization and evaluation  counterfactually this we can try all  sorts of parameters exploration  techniques and by counter factory i mean  we want to estimate the reward of the  policy that we just trained even though  we only collected data  using a logging policy  so once we that allows us to pick a good  candidate we can deploy other customers  and do an a b tests and we can confirm  whether the the new policy uh beats the  the old one and if you want to improve  the the experience we can  let's add a prompt we can go back to  collecting more randomized data and  start again  so i'll focus a little bit on the the  counterfactual evaluation um we use  important sampling also called inverse  propensity propensity scoring  and essentially the idea is that we have  a logged reward from the logging policy  and we can just reweight them using the  probability of a target reaction  according to the target policy divided  by the  pro the action probability according to  the logging policy and with this  rewaiting we get a unbiased estimator uh  which you've probably heard of but um  yeah it's unbiased but it's a fairly  high variance this is all sort of strict  to reduce that variance and in our work  we started using the the sequential  version of this estimator  so what should we optimize for so in our  experience it would make sense to  optimize for task success that is did  the user end up playing something very  recommended at the end of a conversation  however for our first um  our first models we decided to focus on  something that's potentially less noisy  and we call that  prompt usefulness  and so it's a slight tweak on test  success for a prompt to be successful  um not only  does the conversation need to be  successful but also the prompt  needs to yield to an answer from the  user that that's actually useful into  finding the entity that gets played at  the end so for example if we suggest  80's music and the user says yes and you  play 80s music at the end then we get a  usefulness of one if have you played  like 70s music at the end the usefulness  of that prompt would have been zero  so in order to  to  learn what to ask next we need to define  a feature representation for the state  or the context  and we use features characterizing the  information that the user provided  within the conversation the length of  the conversation the the  subscription tier of the user and many  more features we'll see the feature  representation for every action by  action i mean an actual prompt a  candidate prompt and we use  we represent the meaning representation  of the prompt for example the type of  slots being represented like genre era  artists we also have features for the  type of of question being asked whether  it's open-ended or our suggestion or  something else  and also  we  also  have a embedding based affinity feature  that tells us the affinity between the  the user and the entity being suggested  i'll get back to that one later  so once we have our state representation  and our prompt representation the  simplest kind of policies we can we can  build our  reward regressors or value-based  policies where you simply concatenate  those two features and you're trying to  learn a function that predicts 